irika, Philomina Ezeagbor (2004). *Negotiating Power and Privilege: Igbo Career Women in Contemporary Nigeria*. Ohio University Press. p. 34. ISBN 978-0-89680-241-4. Retrieved 24 November 2008.\n186. ↑ Oheneba-Sakyi, Yaw (2006). *African families at the turn of the 21st century*. Greenwood Publishing Group. p. 161. ISBN 978-0-275-97274-5.\n187. ↑ Emeka, Jerry (3 September 2016). \"The Igbo Traditional Attire\". *The Nigerian Voice*. Archived from the original on 5 April 2018. Retrieved 20 April 2019.\n188. 1 2 3 Chuku, Gloria (2005). *Igbo Women and Economic Transformation in Southeastern Nigeria, 1900–1960: 1900–1960*. Routledge. p. 135. ISBN 978-0-415-97210-9. Retrieved 18 December 2008.\n189. 1 2 3 4 5 Masquelier, Adeline Marie (2005). *Dirt, Undress, and Difference: Critical Perspectives on the Body's Surface*. Indiana University Press. pp. 38–45. ISBN 978-0-253-34628-5. Retrieved 18 December 2008.\n190. ↑ Equiano, Olaudah (1837). *The Interesting Narrative of the Life of Olaudah Equiano*. I. Knapp. p. 14.\n191. 1 2 Ukwu, Dele C. \"Igbo People: Clothing & Cosmetic Makeup at the Time of Things Fall Apart\". Archived from the original on 23 September 2008. Retrieved 23 November 2008.\n192. ↑ Isichei, Elizabeth Allo (1977). *Igbo Worlds: An Anthology of Oral Histories and Historical Descriptions*. Macmillan. p. 113. ISBN 978-0-333-19836-0. Retrieved 18 December 2008.\n193. ↑ McCall, John Christensen (2000). *Dancing histories: heuristic ethnography with the Ohafia Igbo*. University of Michigan Press. p. 53. ISBN 978-0-472-11070-4.\n194. ↑ Emenanjọ, E. Nọlue (1978). *Elements of modern Igbo grammar: a descriptive approach*. Oxford University Press. p. 42. ISBN 978-978-154-078-3.\n195. ↑ BBC. \"Yam and the Igbos\". *www.bbc.co.uk*. Retrieved 20 April 2019.\n196. ↑ \"Igbo | people\". *Encyclopedia Britannica*. Archived from the original on 22 May 2020. Retrieved 21 April 2019.\n197. ↑ Agwu, Kene. \"Yam and the Igbos\". BBC Birmingham. Archived from the original on 27 