229-4.\n222. ↑ Taylor, William H. (1996). *Mission to Educate: A History of the Educational Work of the Scottish Presbyterian Mission in East Nigeria, 1846–1960*. BRILL. p. 31. ISBN 978-90-04-10713-7. Retrieved 26 December 2008.\n223. ↑ Pojmann, Wendy Ann (2006). *Immigrant Women and Feminism in Italy*. Ashgate Publishing, Ltd. pp. 35–36. ISBN 978-0-7546-4674-7.\n224. ↑ \"World Igbo Environmental Federation\" (PDF). Archived from the original (PDF) on 19 December 2008. Retrieved 23 November 2008.\n225. ↑ Falola, Toyin; Niyi Afolabi (2008). *Trans-Atlantic Migration: The Paradoxes of Exile*. Routledge. p. 35. ISBN 978-0-415-96091-5.\n226. ↑ \"The Nigerian Diaspora in the United States\" (PDF). *Migration Policy Institute*. 2015. Archived (PDF) from the original on 24 September 2025. Retrieved 6 September 2025.\n227. ↑ Mwakikagile, Godfrey (2004). *Africa Is In A Mess What Went Wrong And What Should Be Done*. Fultus Corporation. p. 82. ISBN 978-0-9744339-7-4.\n228. ↑ Ciment, James (2001). *Encyclopedia of American Immigration*. M.E. Sharpe. p. 1075. ISBN 978-0-7656-8028-0.\n229. ↑ Farr, Marcia (2004). *Ethnolinguistic Chicago: Language and Literacy in the City's Neighborhoods*. Lawrence Erlbaum Associates. p. 182. ISBN 978-0-8058-4345-3.\n230. ↑ Dresser, Norine (2005). *Multicultural Manners: Essential Rules of Etiquette for the 21st Century* (revised, illustrated ed.). John Wiley and Sons. p. 212. ISBN 978-0-471-68428-2.\n231. ↑ Eades, Jeremy Seymour (1993). *Strangers and Traders: Yoruba Migrants, Markets and the State in Northern Ghana* (illustrated ed.). Edinburgh University Press. p. 200. ISBN 978-0-7486-0386-2.\n232. ↑ Minahan, James (2002). *Encyclopedia of the Stateless Nations: S-Z*. Greenwood Publishing Group. p. 330. ISBN 978-0-313-32384-3.\n233. ↑ Richard, Dreux (19 July 2011). \"Japan's Nigerians pay price for prosperity\". *The Japan Times*. Archived from the original on 18 October 2012. Retrieved 31 March 2012.\n234. ↑ Richard, Dreux (11 June 2013). \"J